  "whyItMatters": "A rigid end offset says where the flexible length of a member starts: the part inside the joint (half the depth of the column or beam it frames into) does not bend. It shortens the flexible span and moves the point where the member end forces act on the joint, which adds the moment V x e to the joint. Without it the member spans centre to centre: it is longer and more flexible, and the joint loses the eccentricity moment.",
  "precondition": "a member with a nonzero rigid end offset",
